# Gate step 1252-znbn (assert) — data, not code (order 1072-b7eq).
#
# WHAT IT GUARDS. `behavior_matches_output` in run-litmus-test.sh is a
# natural-language interpreter written in bash `case` arms. `*"succeeds"*`
# means "ignore the output, honour the exit code". `*"multiple"*` means "grep
# the first integer and require >= 2". The fallback is `grep -Fqi`, a LITERAL
# search. So REWORDING AN ENGLISH SENTENCE CHANGES THE RULE THAT DECIDES THE
# VERDICT, and no diff anywhere says the test now checks something else.
#
# THE DEFECT IN ONE MEASUREMENT. Same command, same output, same exit status,
# on the pre-fix runner — only the prose differs:
#
#     command: "echo hello; exit 3"
#     expected_behavior: "the command succeeds"  -> rc=1  FAIL
#     expected_behavior: "hello"                 -> rc=0  PASS
#
# Two verdicts from prose alone. With `assert_exit: 3` declared, all three
# prose variants tested return rc=0: the sentence is inert and the assertion
# decides.
#
# ARM 0 EXISTS BECAUSE THIS FIXTURE'S FIRST DRAFT WAS VOID. The pre-fix control
# is HEAD's runner, and HEAD hardcodes its bindings and tests-dir paths, so it
# could not see the throwaway suite at all — it exited 1 for "spec not found"
# and TWO MEANINGLESS rc=1s READ AS AGREEMENT, i.e. as "prose does not matter",
# the exact opposite of the truth. The control is now equalized by applying
# only the two discovery overrides to it, and ARM 0 asserts the control can
# PASS a case before any later arm reads a verdict from it. A control that
# cannot reach the subject produces confident numbers about nothing.
#
# ARM 4 IS 868-p8xi. An expectation written as an `(a|b)` alternation was
# searched for VERBATIM by the literal fallback, so it could never match and
# litmus:sidecar-arch-derivation failed while printing one of its own listed
# alternatives. `assert_output_matches:` is matched AS a regex. ARM 5 requires
# the same field to still FAIL when no branch matches, because a matcher that
# cannot fail is not a matcher.
#
# ARM 6 IS THE REGRESSION GUARD: a step declaring no `assert_*` adjudicates by
# the legacy path, unchanged.
#
# SCOPE — THIS STEP DOES NOT MIGRATE THE CORPUS. The 64 steps whose
# expected_behavior contains "succeeds", the 10 long sentences and the 4
# wall-clock claims are migrated under the same order but are not what this
# step guards. This guards the MECHANISM: that declaring an assertion makes the
# prose inert.
#
# WHAT IT CANNOT GUARD, stated so the gap is not mistaken for an oversight:
# there is no assert_stdout_* / assert_stderr_* pair, because the runner
# redirects every step `>"$step_capture" 2>&1` and the streams are merged
# before adjudication can see them. Separating them is order 1252-fg9e's
# executor; these fields widen rather than change shape when it lands.
#
# COST, MEASURED HERE 2026-09-18: 0.75 / 0.74 / 0.75 s. It runs a real litmus
# suite eight times against a throwaway corpus in a temp dir — no compile, no
# container, no network, and it never writes into openspec/litmus-tests.
#
# STEP_SCRIPT is a LITERAL path on purpose: a binding assembled from a
# variable is invisible to every name-based scan, including the
# bound-or-retired guard (1063-nraf). Keep it a plain string.
STEP_DESC="Checking a structured litmus assertion makes the prose beside it inert (1252-znbn)"
STEP_SCRIPT="scripts/test-litmus-structured-assert.sh"
STEP_ERROR="a litmus step's English sentence can still change the rule that decides its verdict (1252-znbn) — read which arm failed above; ARM 0 failing means the pre-fix control could not reach the subject and arms 1-2 prove nothing"
STEP_OK="Structured litmus assertions decide their own verdicts"
STEP_SECOND_REGIME="macneo 2026-09-22 darwin ok:litmus-structured-assert:9/9"
